<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sas-Bahu Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1093">1093</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Sahastrabahu Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sas-Bahu Temple is a pair of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples inside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwalior-fort">Gwalior Fort</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and completed in 1093 by the Kachchhapaghata king Mahipala. The larger temple, though its tower has collapsed, is richly carved inside and out. The popular name, &quot;mother-in-law and daughter-in-law&quot;, derives from Sahastrabahu, a name of Vishnu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sas-bahu-temple.jpg" alt="Sas-Bahu Temple" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Airunp · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sas_Bahu_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
